Next, we quantified the performance of the formulas generated by these models on the test sets from 1976 to 2019 using two metrics: (1) the correlation between the formula’s predictions and the measured data within each individual cycle, then these correlations averaged across multiple cycles (avg-R); (2) the correlation across multiple cycles (multi-R). Our simpler formulas substantially outperformed these complex formulas for both metrics. Specifically, the avg-R and multi-R of our method outperform the second-best methods by at least 76.01% and 58.57%, respectively (Fig. 3d). To further validate the accuracy of the formula we obtained, we focused on the SSN data before the 1700s. Due to technological limitations, there are no SSN data directly observed from telescopes before 174943. Therefore, we collected solar modulation levels reconstructed from atmospheric 14C concentrations from the annual rings of thousand-year-old trees as an approximation of the SSN measurements46. We adopted the same smoothing strategy as reported in ref. 46 and found that the Pearson correlation between solar modulation from tree rings and the SSN measured by the telescopes is 0.886 after the 1700s, which verifies their close relationship (Fig. 3f). Then, we compared the SSN generated by our formula and solar modulation data before the 1700s. Their Pearson correlation is 0.561 before 1300, 0.653 from 1300 to 1700 and 0.501 after 1700 (Fig. 3e), which further demonstrates the predictive capacity of our formulas on previously unseen data. Finally, since there are three sine/cosine functions in our formula, it is natural for us to derive three cycles from these trigonometric functions. The shortest cycle is 10.91 yr, which aligns with observations of solar activity widely accepted by the research community. The second longest cycle is 59.27 yr, which coincides exactly with the 60-yr cycle of the ancient Chinese astronomical calendar system of Heavenly Stems and Earthly Branches. The longest cycle is 204.93 yr, which we speculate is the cycle of the solar system operating within a larger planetary system (Fig. 3e). However, as this result is directly derived from symbolic formulas, it requires further confirmation through additional astronomical observational data. The constants of our formula are given in Supplementary Table 2.
Performance of plasma pressure prediction
Plasma pressure is a macroscopic parameter that plays an important role in plasma dynamics and the generation of electric currents. Increasing plasma pressure gradients in the radial direction causes stretching of magnetic field lines and enhances perpendicular currents flowing azimuthally. The azimuthal plasma pressure gradient generates field-aligned currents, resulting in bending of magnetic field lines (Fig. 4a). Wang et al. proposed a formula that describes how equatorial plasma pressure varies with its position relative to Earth using Geotail and the NASA mission Time History of Events and Macroscale Interactions during Substorms data. However, their formula involves exponential terms and nine constants for nightside equatorial isotropic plasma pressure, making it difficult to derive meaningful physical interpretations47,48 (Fig. 4b). To derive a simpler formula, we divided the same equatorial plasma pressure data according to the azimuthal angle, using the data from the near side of the Earth as input for our model and the data from the far side to assess the performance of our formula. As we increased the number of input data points, feeding the PhyE2E model with progressively farther data from Earth, the average mean squared error (MSE) decreased rapidly (Fig. 4c and Supplementary Tables 13 and 14). PhyE2E achieves an average MSE of 7.04 × 10−3 with only 10% of the data provided, demonstrating strong generalization capabilities with the small dataset as input. As more data were provided, the accuracy of our model continued to improve, eventually reaching 6.63 × 10−4, resulting in more precise predictions for the regions on the far side of Earth (Fig. 4e). PhyE2E also outperforms all other baseline models in terms of fitting accuracy (MSE) and model complexity, delivering the most accurate and simplest prediction formula, while other models are unable to provide accurate predictions in certain areas. The formula
by Wang et al. cannot accurately predict the plasma pressure for the regions on the far side of Earth, while the EndToEnd method fails to provide accurate predictions for the regions on the near side of Earth (Fig. 4d). Note that this problem involves two variables, so there are two possible scenarios: one where the distance r from the Earth’s centre and the azimuthal angle φ can be decomposed into two subformulas, and another where decomposition is not possible. We generated one formula for each scenario, compared their MSEs on the training dataset and selected the formula with the lower MSE as the final prediction. In this case, the formula derived using the decomposition method outperformed the alternative. The formula we predicted has a more concise mathematical form compared with the formula proposed by Wang et al. (Fig. 4b). It reveals to us that the decay of the near-Earth plasma pressure is proportional to r2, whereas in the formula proposed by Wang et al. the plasma pressure has an exponential relationship with r. More importantly, we can derive certain physical facts that align with the observational data from this new formula. Specifically, if the plasma pressure decays with the square of r and it is also known that the magnetic pressure decays with the sixth power of r, then, according to the formula plasma beta = magnetic pressure (Pth)/plasma pressure (Pb), we can infer that plasma beta increases with the fourth power of r, which can be confirmed by observational data from another independent study49. Among the formulas obtained by the other methods, only the EndToEnd approach24 produced a formula that is inversely proportional to the square of r. However, its mathematical form is more complex compared with our formula (Supplementary Table 13). The constants of our formula are given in Supplementary Table 3.
Performance of solar rotational angular velocity prediction
The Sun’s magnetic field is generated by the plasma motion within its interior. The angular velocity of solar rotation varies at different latitudes, and the magnetic field lines are stretched and twisted (Fig. 4f). Differential rotation is an important factor in the solar cycle for the prediction of magnetic fields and sunspots. Understanding solar differential rotation helps to improve the prediction of solar activities, which is important for predicting and mitigating the impact of space weather events on satellites and human activities in space. Differential rotation also provides key insights into the structure and dynamics of the solar interior by comparing rotation speeds at different latitudes with those predicted by comprehensive numerical models50,51. One of the most widely adopted formulas describing the relationship between the solar differential rotation and solar latitude was derived by Snodgrass in ‘Magnetic rotation of the solar photosphere’52. In that work, the author assumed that solar differential rotation was symmetric with respect to the equator. Such an assumption often fails, especially during periods of high levels of solar activity. In addition, this formula was fitted by using the measurements at low latitudes of the Sun, but observations at high latitudes are still missing, which limits the suitability of the model near the solar poles. For this task, the challenge for other AI models is that the limited number of data makes it impossible for them to train the model and predict the formulas. PhyE2E derived a simpler and more accurate formula with a simple trigonometric term using the data from Snodgrass52. The largest difference between this formula and the one generated by PhyE2E lies in the difference in the trigonometric periodicity, leading to a steeper prediction for polar regions, rather than a flat one (Fig. 4g). We further reduced the number of training data points and found that PhyE2E could predict the same formula with only 14 data points as input, rapidly achieving an MSE of 1.31 × 10−4, which outperforms other models (Fig. 4g and Supplementary Tables 15 and 16). In contrast to the oscillations seen in other models, PhyE2E exhibits exceptional consistency and robustness, providing stable predictions with varying numbers of input data. The constants of our model can be found in Supplementary Table 4. The predictions for all the baseline models are quite similar in non-polar regions, but they start to diverge in the


