MCTS refinement
The standard MCTS process consists of four steps20: (1) selection, where the best candidate formulas are identified on the basis of their performance; (2) expansion, where new symbolic formulas (which may be incomplete) are generated by adding one more operator from the incomplete formula; (3) simulation, where the incomplete formulas are randomly simulated and then evaluated on the basis of their predictive accuracy; (4) back-propagation, where the results are propagated back to update rewards and visit times of each MCTS node. We employed MCTS to refine the target formula obtained using the end-to-end model. Specifically, random subtrees were removed from the expression tree of the target formula, and MCTS was invoked with the resulting incomplete formula to search for a better target formula. Moreover, following the work of Sun et al.20, we constructed a grammar pool to reduce the search space of MCTS. Specifically, this grammar pool consisted of basic operators (+, −, ×, /, sin(x), cos(x),


